is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch4,switch5,switch6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the Layer‑2 spanning tree domain at Site C (Linux bridge br0 on switch4 with downstream bridges on switch5 and switch6), not router4’s LAN interface.

Mechanism: the access/distribution switches are generating frequent spanning‑tree topology change notifications (TCNs). Each TCN triggers a forwarding database (FDB) flush, briefly disrupting unicast forwarding while MACs are re‑learned. To users this looks like the “default gateway” stops responding for a few seconds even though the router interface never actually flaps.

Localization: 
- Router4 eth0 (192.168.200.1) is stable: link detected yes at 10Gb, no errors/drops, no link up/down events in dmesg, and its MAC (d6:7f:4f:a3:fd:f6) is consistently learned on switch4 eth0 (bridge fdb shows it on eth0). Repeated 100‑probe pings from PC11–PC14 to 192.168.200.1 show 0% loss and sub‑2 ms RTTs.
- Switch4/5/6 show bursts of “br0: topology change detected, propagating” around 18:09–18:12 (direct evidence in dmesg on all three), indicating STP churn in the Site C L2 domain.

Root cause: Repeated STP topology changes in the Site C bridging domain (likely an edge port flap or transient loop on an access port of switch5/6) caused brief forwarding interruptions, not a router4 LAN interface flap or overload.

Corrective fix: Audit Site C cabling for unintended loops; enable RSTP and set all user‑facing ports to edge/portfast with BPDU Guard; identify and remediate the flapping port (use bridge monitoring/logs on switch5/6 to find which interface triggers TCNs). No changes are required on router4; its gateway interface is healthy. 
=== END ===